1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,227
A book swap sounds simple: bring a book, find one you want.

2
00:00:04,227 --> 00:00:06,814
Then you discover its owner doesn't want yours.

3
00:00:06,814 --> 00:00:09,313
A useful trade might need more than two people.

4
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:12,881
We built SwapCircle for that missing connection.

5
00:00:12,881 --> 00:00:16,958
We requested a Qloo API key, but it hadn't arrived when we made

6
00:00:16,958 --> 00:00:17,585
this video.

7
00:00:17,585 --> 00:00:21,231
This is an illustrated walkthrough with authored rankings and

8
00:00:21,231 --> 00:00:22,041
simulated responses.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,426
Six fictional readers bring six books.

10
00:00:25,426 --> 00:00:29,599
Iris and Sam already want each other's, so two people have an

11
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:29,947
exchange.

12
00:00:29,947 --> 00:00:31,384
The other four are waiting.

13
00:00:31,384 --> 00:00:33,500
Nobody has to take an unwanted book.

14
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:38,381
The agent asks Noor about Jules's book, A Psalm for the

15
00:00:38,381 --> 00:00:38,737
Wild-Built.

16
00:00:38,737 --> 00:00:40,981
In this sample, she says yes.

17
00:00:40,981 --> 00:00:43,103
That closes a four-person circle.

18
00:00:43,103 --> 00:00:47,460
Alongside Iris and Sam, six readers now have proposed exchanges.

19
00:00:48,433 --> 00:00:50,491
Wanting a book isn't approving a trade.

20
00:00:50,491 --> 00:00:53,481
Each reader checks the exact book they'll give and receive.

21
00:00:53,481 --> 00:00:55,678
We simulate all six approvals here.

22
00:00:55,678 --> 00:00:58,218
The plan can now produce handoff cards.

23
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:00,419
Then Noor steps out.

24
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:01,749
Her book leaves the table.

25
00:01:01,749 --> 00:01:05,346
The affected circle disappears, and its approvals clear.

26
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:08,798
Iris and Sam keep their unchanged, approved swap.

27
00:01:08,798 --> 00:01:09,903
We're back to two.

28
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:13,651
The agent asks Leo whether he'd read Jules's book.

29
00:01:13,651 --> 00:01:16,750
His sample answer closes a new three-person circle.

30
00:01:16,750 --> 00:01:18,756
Five readers now have proposals.

31
00:01:18,756 --> 00:01:20,983
The changed circle needs fresh approval.

32
00:01:20,983 --> 00:01:23,902
Earlier approval cannot authorize a different trade.

33
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:28,714
With a valid key, the implemented Qloo search would resolve

34
00:01:28,714 --> 00:01:31,022
readers' favorite films, artists and books.

35
00:01:31,022 --> 00:01:34,139
Insights would rank only the other available books for each

36
00:01:34,139 --> 00:01:34,450
reader.

37
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:37,083
That evidence would guide the next reading question.

38
00:01:37,083 --> 00:01:39,919
Real results could produce fewer exchanges.

39
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:43,260
Recommendations never approve trades.

40
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,204
Unknown stays unknown, and failed live requests never substitute

41
00:01:47,204 --> 00:01:48,081
sample data.

42
00:01:48,081 --> 00:01:52,464
Eighty-eight automated tests and 107 checks against the public app

43
00:01:52,464 --> 00:01:52,902
passed.

44
00:01:52,902 --> 00:01:55,883
Those checks used authored data, not live Qloo.

45
00:01:56,833 --> 00:02:00,686
Try the public sample and inspect the MIT-licensed source.

46
00:02:00,686 --> 00:02:03,203
The exchange workflow is available today.

47
00:02:03,203 --> 00:02:05,574
Live Qloo verification remains pending.

48
00:02:05,574 --> 00:02:06,273
Your books.

49
00:02:06,273 --> 00:02:07,077
Your choice.

